 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Polarity",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Pad 1 is the anode (+) and pad 2 the cathode. A green mark sits on the top face at the cathode end, and a green triangle on the underside points toward pad 2."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Pulsed drive",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Up to 60 mA is allowed in pulses of 0.1 ms at a duty of 1/10; continuous current is limited to 30 mA, falling above 25 °C ambient to about 7.5 mA at 85 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Drive",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Run the LED at its rated current with a current-limiting resistor in series: without one, a small change in voltage gives a large change in current that destroys it. Excess forward voltage or a reverse voltage above 5 V, whether in use or in test, damages the part. Avoid surge voltages when the circuit is switched on or off."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Colour and temperature",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "The emitted colour shifts slightly with drive current; a resistor in series with each LED is recommended. Self-heating and a hotter ambient both lower the luminous efficiency and shift the colour, so heat dissipation needs consideration in the design."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Binning",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "LEDs of different bins on one product can show a marked colour difference; keep one bin per product."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Handling",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "The encapsulant is soft: pressure on the lens or the plastic body damages the light-emitting face. Do not touch the lens while it is hot, and after soldering keep force, vibration and board bending away from the part."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Soldering",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Reflow follows the lead-free profile, no more than twice, with an easy-to-clean flux; any residue on the lens changes the colour. Hand soldering is for repair or rework only: at most 300 °C for at most 3 s, once, with an iron of no more than 25 W."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Cleaning",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Ultrasonic cleaning is not allowed. Wipe with, or dip for no more than 1 minute in, isopropyl alcohol or pure alcohol, then leave 15 minutes at room temperature before use. Thinner, trichloroethylene, acetone, sulphides, nitrides, acids, alkalis and salts attack the LED."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Potting and adhesives",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Volatile substances that get into the LED discolour it under light and heat and cut its output sharply; test every material for compatibility, and do not mount it with adhesives that give off organic vapour. With a normal potting compound, first light a small batch for 168 hours at room temperature."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Storage and moisture",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Sealed, store at 30 °C or below and under 60% RH and use within a year. The part is moisture sensitive: once opened, use it within 7 days at 30 °C or below and 60% RH or below, since absorbed moisture can crack the encapsulant in reflow. Unused parts are dried, reels at 60 ± 5 °C for 24 h and loose parts at 105 ± 5 °C for 1 h, then resealed in a foil bag or kept in a nitrogen cabinet. Keep acids, alkalis, corrosive gases, strong vibration and strong magnetic fields away."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Electrostatic discharge",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Static and surge voltages damage the LED; use wrist straps, gloves and shoes rated for static and grounded equipment. A damaged LED leaks noticeably more in reverse, has a lower forward voltage at low current, and may stay dark at low current."
  }
 ],
